# Volume 1 — Construct Boundaries

**Status:** normative computational design theory  
**Sensory and physical conclusions:** `NOT TESTED`

## Construct

Perfume depth is not one variable. It is a family of questions asked at
different levels of the odor system. The first duty of a serious formulation
method is to keep those levels separate long enough to reason about them.
Collapsing them creates false confidence: a long formula is called complex, a
persistent base is called deep, a wide modeled OAV range is called dimensional,
or a pleasant material list is called hedonic. None of those substitutions is
scientifically or professionally adequate.

**Construction complexity** describes the manufactured object: number of
formula rows, stock preparations, modules, preblends, natural materials,
concentration levels, and process steps. It matters for measurability,
repeatability, inventory, manufacturing risk, and the possibility of padding
or compression. A natural material counts as one formula identity even though
it contains many molecules. Its internal molecular population may matter to
analysis, but imagining those constituents does not create extra independently
designed formula roles.

**Molecular complexity** can mean structural complexity of one molecule or the
number and diversity of volatile molecules in a mixture. Kermen and colleagues
reported relationships between the structural complexity of individual
odorants, the number of reported notes, and pleasantness. That result is about
single-molecule structure and must not be converted into a formula-row rule.
An EO or absolute may contain hundreds of molecules, yet its odor can function
as one highly integrated object. Conversely, two chemicals at a critical ratio
can form an emergent object richer than either component.

**Physical-signal complexity** describes what reaches the air and receptor
environment: concentrations, vapor pressures, partitioning, activity
coefficients, matrix interactions, evaporation, permeation, plume intermittency,
and changing component ratios. This layer can be modeled and measured, but it
does not by itself reveal the resulting odor quality. OAV is useful here as a
threshold-relative diagnostic. It is not a depth meter.

**Perceptual-facet complexity** describes how many attributes can be
reliably perceived and reproduced under a defined protocol. A formula can
contain many chemicals while presenting only one generic facet because of
masking, blending, adaptation, or common character. It can also present a
coherent object whose internal qualities are sensed but whose chemical
components cannot be named. Human mixture work repeatedly shows limited
component identification, often around three or four odor objects, even after
training. That limit is not a formula cap; it means depth must be designed as
organized perception rather than a demand that every row announce itself.

**Configural complexity** concerns the integrity of the whole. Some mixtures
are processed primarily as their recognizable components, while others create
a new odor object. A configural perfume can be internally rich without
decomposing into a list during one sniff. The relevant questions are whether
the whole is named, whether its identity survives time and controlled omission,
and whether internal distinctions enrich rather than split it.

**Relational complexity** describes dependencies: anchor, bridge, echo,
contrast, suppression control, temporal handoff, recurrence, and n-ary
cooperation. It is often the missing element in shallow high-count formulas.
Thirty materials assigned to thirty isolated adjectives do not create thirty
relations. A relationship exists only when changing one participant changes
the target-linked behavior of another or of the whole in a predictable,
testable way.

**Textural complexity** describes differentiated materiality: satin versus
wax, dry grain versus cream, suede nap versus polished hide, wet stone versus
dust, pulp versus skin. Texture is crossmodal and semantic, not literal touch.
It must therefore be measured as an odor-evoked attribute, separately from
intensity and liking.

**Temporal complexity** describes states and transitions: entry, revelation,
handoff, recurrence, metamorphosis, decay, and identity retention. Merely
lasting is not temporal depth. A flat persistent chord has duration but little
state differentiation. A short perfume can have meaningful temporal structure
if its changes are coherent and target-linked.

**Spatial complexity** describes perceived contour and actual delivery:
proximity, halo, lift, trail, enclosure, center, boundary, and persistence at
distance. Some of this language is metaphorical and some reflects physical
concentration gradients. The doctrine keeps spatial sensory reports separate
from vapor calculations.

**Hedonic complexity** describes the organization of reward, tension, relief,
comfort, surprise, familiarity, novelty, satiation, and desire to re-smell.
Liking is an endpoint, not a synonym for depth. A perfume may be deep and
disliked, simple and intensely liked, or initially attractive but fatiguing.

**Cognitive complexity** includes expertise, labels, memory, culture,
expectation, and learned category boundaries. Experts may extract different
elements from a mixture without escaping human processing limits. Familiarity
can alter pleasantness nonlinearly. These effects require assessor-level data,
not assumptions about a universal nose.

**Evidence complexity** records what kind of support exists: hypothesis,
closed experiment design, formula-bound observation, replicated observation,
or external literature with limited transfer. Evidence richness is not perfume
depth, but inadequate evidence prevents an honest depth claim.

## Mechanisms

The mapping from construction to perception is many-to-many and nonlinear.
Adding one material may be dormant, consciously perceptible, modulatory,
masking, synergistic, configural, identity-breaking, temporally delayed, or
assessor-specific. Removing one may expose another through reduced suppression.
Changing a ratio below the just-noticeable difference of an isolated component
may still alter the typicality of the blended object. The correct conceptual
model is therefore a vector of causal changes, not a monotonically increasing
count.

A working, explicitly unvalidated hypothesis is that useful perceptual
richness often has a nonmonotonic relationship with construction expansion.
Early target-linked additions can resolve anatomy or relations. Later additions
may provide microtexture or robustness. Redundant additions eventually produce
no reproducible difference, masking, or convergence toward a generic object.
The location and even existence of that turning point are formula-specific.
The olfactory-white experiment demonstrates convergence under equal-intensity,
broadly distributed conditions; it does not establish a universal thirty-row
limit for perfume.

Mechanisms must be stated at the correct level. “Adds depth” is not a mechanism.
“A dry bitter trace increases separation between sweet floral flesh and creamy
wood, tested by constant-total omission and ratio arms” is a mechanism
hypothesis. It names participants, direction, target benefit, failure, and a
way to be wrong.

## Material translation

A known chemical can function as a recognizer, modifier, bridge, contrast,
texture carrier, temporal scheduler, diffusion support, persistence support,
or technical material. An EO or absolute can perform the same roles as a whole
odor object while its constituents support composite-OAV diagnostics. A
carrier can change delivery geometry without carrying an intended odor facet.
A fixative can alter release without adding sensory depth. A musk can shape
skin continuity or diffusion, but adding multiple musks does not automatically
increase dimensionality and may produce redundant fog or genotype-dependent
false absence.

The material class does not determine the function. A citrus-coded material
may persist and bridge a drydown; a nominal base material may project early; a
floral material may provide wood polish; a bitter leather trace may define a
flower's shadow. Function is target- and dose-specific. Inventory ownership
establishes feasibility, not relevance. TARGET/IDEAL retains an exact missing
function even when CURRENT-INVENTORY cannot realize it.

## Interactions

Constructs influence one another but cannot compensate automatically.
Temporal change can reveal anatomy; texture contrast can strengthen identity;
physical persistence can permit recurrence; familiarity can alter liking.
Yet persistence cannot compensate for missing identity, and liking cannot
prove realism. A rich comparison therefore reports interactions explicitly:
which physical changes are expected to alter which perceptual dimensions,
which relations depend on time, and which hedonic endpoint might respond.

Interaction claims require attention to ratio and context. Receptor
antagonism, mixture suppression, central configural processing, and adaptation
mean that a component's isolated smell or OAV cannot specify its mixture role.
Pairwise evidence also cannot automatically establish an n-ary interaction.
When three or more participants are claimed to create a special effect, the
complete combination needs its own recombination or factorial test.

## Failure modes

The central failures are category errors. Construction count masquerades as
perception. Darkness masquerades as depth. Persistence masquerades as temporal
development. OAV masquerades as intensity, then intensity masquerades as
liking. Material descriptors masquerade as finished-mixture attributes.
Literature about one molecule or one binary mixture is transferred to a
different concentration and matrix without verification.

Other failures include decorative roles with no omission loss, many rows tied
to one generic chassis, microtexture exceeding what the protocol can resolve,
unbalanced references, unblinded preference judgments, formula changes during
evaluation, and assessor averages that hide opposite responses. A design can
also fail by overreacting against padding: “precise simplicity” becomes an
excuse to delete required anatomy and interfaces from an explicitly
high-resolution target.

## Observables

Record each construct separately. Construction observables include row count,
active count, preparation count, natural count, modules, and measurable dose
geometry. Physical observables include active ppm, vapor estimates, ODT source,
OAV state, time-window concentrations, evaporation, and application mass.
Perceptual observables include named identity, recognizer presence, attribute
applicability, hierarchy, genericization, texture terms, and temporal state.
Spatial observables include detection distance under a defined protocol,
proximity, halo, trail identity, and persistence. Hedonic observables include
liking, preference, desire to re-smell, fatigue, and assessor-level ties.

Do not merge missing values into zero. `UNKNOWN`, `NOT APPLICABLE`, and `NOT
TESTED` are different states. Report participant distributions and repeated
responses where possible; an average can conceal anosmia, opposing preference,
or order effects.

## Controlled tests

Begin with an exact target and frozen formula. Use carrier-matched,
constant-total ablation to ask whether one material or module contributes its
declared function. Use low/target/high ratio arms when the uncertainty is dose
or relationship shape. Use recombination arms for modules and factorial or
selected n-ary designs for claimed interactions. Counterbalance order, blind
codes, repeat controls, and evaluate predetermined time windows.

Measure identity, facet richness, texture, temporal continuity, spatial
behavior, genericization, and liking separately. A material can win one
endpoint and lose another. Predeclare accept and reject rules; do not retain an
addition because the evaluator can invent a new rationale after smelling it.
When no target-linked deficiency exists, `NO_CHANGE` is a valid result.

## Claim boundary

This volume supplies ontology and experiment logic. It cannot determine how
any formula smells. No row count, graph, OAV distribution, model output, or
documented role establishes sensory depth, luxury, realism, liking,
performance, similarity, safety, stability, or release. Those remain `NOT
TESTED` until exact-formula physical evidence exists.

## Primary literature

- Laing DG, Francis GW. *The capacity of humans to identify odors in
  mixtures.* Physiology & Behavior 46 (1989), 809–814.
  https://doi.org/10.1016/0031-9384(89)90041-3
- Livermore A, Laing DG. *Influence of training and experience on the
  perception of multicomponent odor mixtures.* Journal of Experimental
  Psychology: Human Perception and Performance 22 (1996), 267–277.
  https://doi.org/10.1037/0096-1523.22.2.267
- Kermen F et al. *Molecular complexity determines the number of olfactory
  notes and the pleasantness of smells.* Scientific Reports 1 (2011), 206.
  https://doi.org/10.1038/srep00206
- Weiss T et al. *Perceptual convergence of multi-component mixtures in
  olfaction implies an olfactory white.* PNAS 109 (2012), 19959–19964.
  https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.1208110109
- Le Berre E et al. *Just noticeable differences in component concentrations
  modify the odor quality of a blending mixture.* Chemical Senses 33 (2008),
  389–395. https://doi.org/10.1093/chemse/bjn006

